state management and set state in class based components
  
constructor function-
   the constructor is called during component creation and before mounting.
it is a method which is used to initialize an objects state in a class.
the concept of a constructor is same in react.
it can also be used to bind event handlers.

setstate- it is a method used to update the state of a component.

the state in js object holds the dynamic data of the component and determines how the component renders and behaves . When the state changes , React re-renders the component to reflect the updated states.

Here's an overview of how setState works:

Basic Usage:
You can pass an object to setState to update the component's state. React will merge the new state with the current state.

javascript
Copy code
this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
Functional Form:
When you need to update the state based on the previous state, it's recommended to use the functional form of setState. This form ensures that you are working with the most current state, which is particularly important in asynchronous updates.

javascript
Copy code
this.setState((prevState) => ({
  count: prevState.count + 1
}));
Partial State Updates:
setState only updates the specified properties in the state object and leaves the other properties unchanged. For example, if your state has multiple properties, updating one property won't affect the others.

javascript
Copy code
this.setState({ count: 10 }); // Only updates 'count', other state properties remain the same
Asynchronous Nature:
setState may be asynchronous, meaning that multiple setState calls may be batched together for performance improvements. Due to this, you shouldn't rely on the state immediately after calling setState.

javascript
Copy code
// Incorrect: This might not give the expected result due to batching
this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 });
console.log(this.state.count); // This might log the old state

// Correct: Using the callback form ensures you work with the latest state
this.setState((prevState) => ({ count: prevState.count + 1 }), () => {
  console.log(this.state.count); // This logs the updated state
});
Callback Function:
setState can accept a second argument, which is a callback function that gets executed after the state has been updated and the component has re-rendered.

javascript
Copy code
this.setState({ count: this.state.count + 1 }, () => {
  console.log('State updated:', this.state.count);
});
Example
Here's a simple example that illustrates how setState works in a React component:

javascript
Copy code
import React, { Component } from 'react';

class Counter extends Component {
  constructor() {
    super();
    this.state = {
      count: 0,
    };
  }

  increment = () => {
    this.setState((prevState) => ({
      count: prevState.count + 1
    }));
  }

  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        <button onClick={this.increment}>Increment</button>
        <p>Count: {this.state.count}</p>
      </div>
    );
  }
}

export default Counter;
In this example:

The initial state is set to { count: 0 }.
The increment method updates the state using the functional form of setState.
Each time the button is clicked, the increment method is called, updating the state and re-rendering the component to display the new count.

if we will not use arrow function here, then the function will be called directly and will not wait for the button to get clicked. 
  increment call ho rha  -> set update ho rhi -> re-render ho rha and then the process continue so it will go in infinite loop

